# Copy to `.env` and fill in. Nothing here is required to run the README quickstart —
# it uses an in-memory repository and a local folder, both of which need no configuration.
#
# Two kinds of variable live in this file:
#   MEDIA_*  are read by @node-media-library/core itself, and ONLY when you omit the
#            `storage` key from createMediaLibrary() entirely. Pass `storage.disks` and
#            these are ignored (MEDIA_PREFIX excepted — it still backs `storage.prefix`).
#   everything else is read by Prisma, BullMQ, RabbitMQ, or a cloud SDK. This library
#            never reads them; you pass them into your own config.


# ─── Storage: pick ONE block ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Precedence when `storage` is omitted: R2 → S3 → GCS → local fs.
# Whichever block you fill in wins over the ones below it.

# Cloudflare R2. MEDIA_R2_ACCOUNT_ID without MEDIA_R2_BUCKET throws at startup rather
# than silently falling through to another driver.
# MEDIA_R2_ACCOUNT_ID=your-account-id
# MEDIA_R2_BUCKET=example-media
# MEDIA_R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# MEDIA_R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# R2 has no object ACLs, so public files need an r2.dev subdomain or a custom domain.
# Without this, url() throws for public reads — use signedUrl() instead.
# MEDIA_R2_BASE_URL=https://cdn.example.com

# Amazon S3, or any S3-compatible endpoint (MinIO, Backblaze, DigitalOcean Spaces).
# Credentials come from the AWS SDK's default chain — see AWS_* below.
# MEDIA_S3_BUCKET=example-media
# MEDIA_S3_REGION=us-east-1
# MEDIA_S3_ENDPOINT=

# Google Cloud Storage. Credentials come from GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS below.
# MEDIA_GCS_BUCKET=example-media

# Local filesystem. The fallback when none of the above are set.
# Note this disk cannot produce URLs without a `baseUrl`, which has no env var —
# set it in `storage.disks` config. See README step 4.
# MEDIA_FS_ROOT=./storage/media

# Prefixes every stored path, on any driver. Unlike the rest, this one applies even
# when you configure `storage` explicitly (it backs `storage.prefix`).
# MEDIA_PREFIX=

# Set to `production` and core warns on startup if media is still on a local fs disk.
# NODE_ENV=development


# ─── Cloud credentials (read by the SDKs, not by this library) ──────────────────
# Only needed if you don't pass `credentials` on the disk config yourself. The AWS
# SDK's default chain also covers IAM roles and instance profiles, which are better
# than static keys wherever they're available.

# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS=/path/to/service-account.json


# ─── Your own wiring (read by Prisma / BullMQ / RabbitMQ) ───────────────────────
# The library never reads these. You read them and pass the result into
# createMediaLibrary(), e.g. `queue: bullmqDriver({ connection: { url: process.env.REDIS_URL } })`.

# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/app
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
# AMQP_URL=amqp://guest:guest@localhost:5672


# ─── Not here on purpose ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The unprefixed R2_ACCOUNT_ID / R2_BUCKET / R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID / R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY /
# R2_BASE_URL variables gate this repo's own live-R2 test suite. They are CI secrets for
# contributors, NOT runtime configuration — an application sets MEDIA_R2_* instead.
# Likewise REDIS_URL and AMQP_URL gate the adapter integration suites. See CONTRIBUTING.md.
